# The TX15 on the flight computer's serial line, as on the real plane (domains/aviation). The
# station starts the sim with `--flight-computer`, and a simulated TX15 sends the sticks and
# switches down the line. The flight computer turns them into the radio channels of ArduPilot,
# and sends the telemetry back to the TX15, which shows it on its screen. The sim writes that
# screen to a file that the console's driver reads (--tx15-screen).

Section: The TX15 on the serial line
  On the real plane, the TX15 is not a USB game controller. It sends its sticks and switches to
  the [[flight computer]] on a serial line, in the protocol of ExpressLRS. The flight computer
  gives ArduPilot the radio channels. ArduPilot sends its state back on the same line, and the
  TX15 shows it on its screen. The lamps along the top of the screen show the flight mode,
  whether the plane is armed, GPS, the link and the battery.

  Start the station with `--flight-computer`, and the sim ignores the USB controller. A
  simulated TX15 on the serial line takes its place, so that the plane reacts as it does with
  the real radio. These tutorials show the radio the way you use it at the field.

Background:
  Given the console is connected to a VTOL on the ground
  And the TX15 is on the serial line

Vocabulary:
  Phrase: the TX15 is on the serial line
    Say: start the station with `--flight-computer`. The simulated TX15 is on the serial line. Put the throttle stick all the way down, SE out and press button 1
    Given the radio line is serial
    And the throttle stick is low
    And the arm switch is off
    And the mode switch is QHOVER
    And the TX15 screen arming is disarmed
